# tui_event.cyr — the Linux TUI event loop's epoll-record read (v0.11.0).
#
# WHY ITS OWN FILE. tests/chakshu.tcyr cannot include src/tui.cyr — it pulls in
# the render path, mihi, the snapshot helpers and the AI stub — so anything that
# lives only there is reachable from the PTY suite alone, which runs on x86_64.
# This is the one piece of the event loop whose correctness depends on the CPU
# architecture, so it is split out where the unit suite can run it on every arch
# the toolchain targets. Same split, same reason, as rate.cyr (v0.9.4).
#
# THE LAYOUT. `struct epoll_event` is `{ u32 events; u64 data; }`, and its
# layout is per architecture:
#
#   x86_64   declared packed (EPOLL_PACKED)   data at +4, record 12 bytes
#   aarch64  natural alignment                data at +8, record 16 bytes
#
# The stdlib's `epoll_event_new` writes each arch's layout
# (lib/syscalls_x86_64_linux.cyr, lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr); it has no
# reader, so this is it.
#
# ⛔ THE DEFECT THIS FIXES. Through v0.10.3 tui_run read the tag with
# `load64(&events + 4)` on every arch. On aarch64 that loads the four bytes of
# padding after `events` plus the low half of `data`, so no wakeup ever matched
# its tag and every one fell through to the stdin branch: an exit signal or a
# resize was handled as a keystroke, and the blocking read(0) that followed
# waited for a real key — `kill` did nothing until someone typed, and a resize
# was never repainted. Found by darshana's consumer survey (2026-10-12); cyim
# gates the same read per arch.

# The `data` field of the first `struct epoll_event` at `ev`, as filled in by
# epoll_wait(2). Linux only — agnos has no epoll and its TUI loop polls instead.
fn tui_epoll_data(ev) {
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
    return load64(ev + 8);
    #endif
    #ifndef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
    return load64(ev + 4);
    #endif
}
